---
const houses = [
  {
    name: "The Booth House",
    address: "265 Sylvan Road",
    year: "1915–16",
    note: "The centerpiece of the development — three stories plus a rooftop deck, four fireplaces, and the only Wright house in the collection with a square motif carried through every detail from structural bays to door knobs.",
    isCurrent: true,
  },
  {
    name: "Charles R. Perry House",
    address: "272 Sylvan Road",
    year: "1915–16",
    note: "Shares the Prairie vocabulary of the four Meadow Road houses — low hipped roof, deep overhangs, stucco cladding — but occupies a prominent corner lot at the development's entrance.",
  },
  {
    name: "Lute F. &amp; Daniel Kissam House",
    address: "1023 Meadow Road",
    year: "1915–16",
    note: "One of four speculatively built houses sharing the floor plan of Wright's 1907 Ladies' Home Journal design 'A Fireproof House for $5,000.' The compact two-story plan measures 30 feet square, with identical facades on all four sides and a central stair.",
  },
  {
    name: "William F. Ross House",
    address: "1027 Meadow Road",
    year: "1915–16",
    note: "The one house that departs from the standard Fireproof House plan — the fireplace is repositioned to the side, giving the living spaces a different orientation. Purchased by architect John Eifler and meticulously restored between 2011 and 2014.",
  },
  {
    name: "William F. Kier House",
    address: "1031 Meadow Road",
    year: "1915–16",
    note: "Another of the four Fireproof House variants. The consistent rooflines and material palette along Meadow Road give the Meadow Road houses a unified Prairie streetscape that Wright intended as a coherent residential ensemble.",
  },
  {
    name: "Hollis R. Root House",
    address: "1030 Meadow Road",
    year: "1915–16",
    note: "The fourth of the identical-plan houses. Together, the four Meadow Road houses demonstrate how Wright could use a single repeated plan to create a neighborhood rather than a collection of individual commissions.",
  },
  {
    name: "Ravine Bluffs Bridge &amp; Sculptures",
    address: "Sylvan Road",
    year: "1915–16",
    note: "An 80-foot reinforced concrete bridge spanning the ravine — one of only two freestanding bridges Wright ever designed. Three matching concrete sculptures with built-in lamp standards and planting urns mark the development entrance. The original bridge was demolished in 1984 and rebuilt as a faithful replica the following year.",
    isBridge: true,
  },
];
---

<section class="ravine" id="ravine">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="ravine-header">
      <p class="section-label">Neighborhood</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Ravine Bluffs Development</h2>
      <div class="divider"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="ravine-body">
      <div class="ravine-text">
        <p>
          The Booth House does not stand alone. It is the anchor of the Ravine Bluffs Development —
          an ambitious residential project that Sherman Booth conceived for a wooded tract of land
          along Sylvan Road in Glencoe, overlooking the ravines that give the neighborhood its name.
          Glencoe is home to the <strong>third-largest concentration of Frank Lloyd Wright
          structures</strong> in the state of Illinois: seven houses, a bridge, and three sculptural
          entrance markers — all designed in a single concentrated period between 1913 and 1916.
        </p>
        <p>
          Booth engaged Wright to design not only his own house but five additional homes,
          a concrete bridge, and three monumental entrance sculptures — a remarkable act of patronage
          that produced one of the most cohesive collections of Prairie architecture anywhere in the
          world. The four houses on Meadow Road share the same basic floor plan, derived from
          Wright's 1907 <em>Ladies' Home Journal</em> design "A Fireproof House for $5,000" — a
          30-foot-square, two-story plan with identical facades on all sides. The Ross House departs
          from the template, with a repositioned fireplace that gives it a subtly different character.
        </p>
        <p>
          These houses are still commonly described as small, low-cost rental units — part of Wright's
          long effort to build for people of moderate means. The documentary record does not support
          it.<sup><a href="#fn-19" class="fn-ref">19</a></sup> The subdivision was platted on
          August 4, 1914 and marketed from July 1915 as a "new high class" neighborhood on Glencoe's
          lakefront, advertised in the Sunday <em>Chicago Tribune</em> with Wright's name set in
          capitals, "unique artistic entrance features," a "specially designed artistic electric
          railway station," and lots to be individually planted by Jens Jensen. The four building
          permits issued on October 28, 1915 put each house at $7,500 — more than twice the $3,500
          national average that year, and at the top of the range of the sixty-nine permits Glencoe
          issued in 1915. At roughly 1,800 square feet they ran above the 1,500-square-foot national
          average as well. They were built to be sold, not rented, by H. A. Peters and Company, the
          contractor Booth engaged for the entire development — roads, utilities, bridge and houses
          alike.
        </p>
        <p>
          What followed was a failure of timing rather than of design. One house sold at once. Then
          American taste began turning away from the Prairie style, war in Europe drained consumer
          confidence, and when the United States entered the war in April 1917 four houses still
          stood unsold. Booth's own family bought three of them. The last was rented out — the single
          circumstance from which the "rental houses" description appears to descend — until it
          finally sold on September 1, 1921, nearly six years after the first lots were offered.
        </p>
        <p>
          The bridge spanning the ravine at Sylvan Road is one of only two freestanding bridges
          Wright ever designed. The original 1915 structure — 80 feet long and 30 feet wide,
          reinforced concrete with built-in bench seating, lamp standards, and broad planting urns
          at each end — was demolished in 1984 and immediately rebuilt as a faithful replica by
          the Village of Glencoe. Three matching poured-concrete sculptures with integrated
          street lamps mark the development's entrance; all three survive.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Ravine Bluffs Development was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in
          1978 (NRHP reference #78001137), recognized as an outstanding example of Wright's domestic
          work and of early twentieth-century planned residential development. Walking Sylvan Road
          and Meadow Road today, the relationship between the houses, the landscape, and Wright's
          bridge creates an experience that exists nowhere else — an intact Prairie neighborhood,
          lived in and evolving, exactly as Wright intended.
        </p>
        <p>
          Glencoe also holds an earlier Wright commission: the William A. Glasner House at
          850 Sheridan Road (1905), a one-story Prairie cottage perched on the edge of a ravine
          and celebrated for its two octagonal rooms and its "Tree of Life" leaded-glass casement
          windows. Together with the Ravine Bluffs Development, the Glasner House makes Glencoe
          home to the <strong>third-largest concentration of Frank Lloyd Wright structures</strong>
          in the world, after Oak Park and Chicago.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Map -->
      <figure class="ravine-map">
        <a href="/images/ravine/map.jpg" class="glightbox" data-description="Ravine Bluffs, Glencoe, Illinois — plat map showing the Booth development lots, Sylvan Road, Meadow Road, and the surrounding neighborhood. Landscaping by Jens Jensen. Sherman M. Booth, Owner.">
          <img src="/images/ravine/map.jpg" alt="Ravine Bluffs Development plat map, Glencoe, Illinois" />
        </a>
        <figcaption>Ravine Bluffs plat map · Landscaping by Jens Jensen · Sherman M. Booth, Owner</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- House index -->
    <div class="ravine-index">
      <h3 class="ravine-index-title">The Collection</h3>
      <div class="house-grid">
        {houses.map(house => (
          <div class={`house-card ${house.isCurrent ? 'house-card--current' : ''} ${house.isBridge ? 'house-card--bridge' : ''}`}>
            {house.isCurrent && <span class="house-badge">This House</span>}
            <div class="house-card-img">
              <img
                src={house.isCurrent ? '/images/hero/hero.jpg' : `/images/ravine/${house.address.replace(/\s+/g, '-').toLowerCase()}.jpg`}
                alt={house.name}
                loading="lazy"
                onerror="this.parentElement.classList.add('no-image')"
              />
            </div>
            <div class="house-card-body">
              <span class="house-year">{house.year}</span>
              <h4 class="house-name">{house.name}</h4>
              <p class="house-address">{house.address}</p>
              <p class="house-note">{house.note}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .ravine {
    background: var(--color-bg-dark);
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }

  .ravine .section-label {
    color: var(--color-accent-light);
  }

  .ravine .section-title {
    color: var(--color-white);
  }

  .ravine .divider {
    background: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .ravine-header {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-lg);
  }

  .ravine-body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 340px;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    align-items: start;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .ravine-text p {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  /* Map */
  .ravine-map {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .ravine-map a {
    display: block;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);
    cursor: zoom-in;
  }

  .ravine-map img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
    transition: transform 0.5s ease, opacity 0.3s;
    opacity: 0.9;
  }

  .ravine-map a:hover img {
    transform: scale(1.03);
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .ravine-map figcaption {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.35);
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* House index */
  .ravine-index-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--color-white);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    padding-top: var(--space-lg);
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);
  }

  .house-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .house-card {
    position: relative;
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.04);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);
    transition: border-color 0.2s, background 0.2s;
  }

  .house-card:hover {
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.07);
    border-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15);
  }

  .house-card--current {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .house-card--current:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-accent-light);
  }

  .house-badge {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.75rem;
    right: 0.75rem;
    z-index: 1;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: white;
    padding: 0.2em 0.6em;
  }

  .house-card-img {
    aspect-ratio: 4/3;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.05);
  }

  .house-card-img img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    opacity: 0.85;
    transition: opacity 0.3s, transform 0.5s;
  }

  .house-card:hover .house-card-img img {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: scale(1.03);
  }

  /* When image fails to load */
  .house-card-img.no-image {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .house-card-img.no-image::after {
    content: "Photo coming soon";
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.3);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .house-card-img.no-image img {
    display: none;
  }

  .house-card-body {
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }

  .house-year {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--color-accent-light);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .house-name {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--color-white);
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0.1rem;
  }

  .house-address {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.4);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .house-note {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 300;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .ravine-body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

  }
</style>
